== Logic / deduction audit ==

[[Logic]] is not reduced to classical logic.

The page separates:

* syntax;
* proof systems;
* semantics;
* consequence relations.

Soundness/completeness are treated as system properties, not universal givens.

[[Deductive reasoning]] keeps:

<pre>
validity ≠ premise truth
soundness = validity + true premises
</pre>

and separates formal consequence from psychological confidence.

== Induction / abduction audit ==

[[Inductive reasoning]] remains broader than enumerative induction and explicitly includes statistical, predictive, analogical, and probabilistic support.

The problem of induction is presented as an epistemological challenge rather than “solved” by probability theory.

[[Abductive reasoning]] is explicitly explanatory/ampliative.

Inference to the best explanation is central but does not guarantee truth, and Bayesian connections are presented as relations rather than identity.

== Argument / proof / conjecture audit ==

[[Argument]] separates logical structure from rhetorical conflict and from explanation.

[[Proof]] distinguishes rigorous mathematical proof from empirical confirmation and from fully formal derivation.

[[Conjecture]] distinguishes current epistemic status from formal independence.

A drafting error was corrected before Phase 3: Technical depth no longer uses <code>T ⊬ φ</code> as shorthand for merely “no proof is currently known”.

== Contradiction / consistency audit ==

[[Contradiction]] distinguishes:

* contradiction from ordinary disagreement;
* classical explosion from paraconsistent alternatives;
* proof-by-contradiction from the definition of contradiction.

[[Consistency (logic)]] distinguishes:

* syntactic consistency;
* semantic satisfiability;
* truth;
* completeness;
* relative consistency.

The equivalence between consistency and satisfiability is restricted to the relevant sound/complete classical first-order setting rather than asserted for every logic.

== Probability audit ==

[[Probability]] separates mathematical axioms from interpretations.

A drafting issue was corrected before Phase 3: Simple depth explicitly states that probability 0 does not imply logical impossibility in continuous models, and probability 1 does not imply logical necessity.

[[Probability distribution]] distinguishes:

* mass from density;
* density values from point probabilities;
* theoretical distributions from empirical histograms/data summaries.

== Correlation audit ==

[[Correlation]] is not reduced to Pearson's coefficient.

The page preserves:

<pre>
correlation ≠ causation
zero Pearson correlation ≠ independence
</pre>

and notes nonlinear dependence, rank/dependence alternatives, outliers, range restriction, and measurement/context sensitivity.

== Conditional probability audit ==

[[Conditional probability]] correctly defines:

<pre>
P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B)
</pre>

when P(B)>0 and explicitly preserves directionality:

<pre>
P(A|B) ≠ P(B|A)
</pre>

It also separates observational conditioning from causal intervention.

== Bayesian inference audit ==

[[Bayesian inference]] separates:

* prior;
* likelihood;
* posterior;
* posterior prediction;
* model comparison;
* computation;
* model checking.

Bayes' theorem is presented as a probability identity inside, but not equivalent to, the whole inference workflow.

Priors are not described as arbitrary by definition.

Precise posterior output is not treated as a guarantee of model adequacy.

== Belief revision audit ==

[[Belief revision]] uses AGM as the foundational framework while explicitly preserving broader field scope:

* expansion;
* contraction;
* revision;
* belief sets versus bases;
* iterated change;
* prioritized versus non-prioritized revision;
* probabilistic connections.

Full-belief revision is not equated with Bayesian conditionalization or human memory change.

== Formal-logic audit ==

[[Propositional logic]] is restricted to sentence-level variables/connectives and points outward to first-order expressiveness.

[[First-order logic]] makes the first-order boundary explicit:

<pre>
quantification over individuals
≠ quantification over predicates/properties
</pre>

Its central metalogical properties are described without implying decidability.

[[Modal logic]] is presented as a field of systems rather than one axiom set.

Kripke semantics is central but not treated as the only conceivable semantic framework.

Necessity/possibility remain distinct from probability.

== Public concept versus AI/BITwiki application ==

The wave preserves:

<pre>
argument           ≠ hidden chain-of-thought
proof              ≠ software test result by definition
probability        ≠ model confidence score by default
Bayesian inference ≠ any iterative update
belief revision    ≠ agent memory mutation
first-order logic  ≠ ontology schema
modal logic        ≠ workflow state machine
</pre>

No local agent or BITwiki mechanism is used as the definition of a public concept.

